  "query_preview": "..The main weather story over the weekend and into next \nweek will be the increasing heat across southeast Arizona. In the \nmid to upper levels, ridging continues to build in this weekend with \nincreasing heights and thickness resulting in an upward trend in \ntemperatures. Highs are expected to peak on Monday at 10 to 16 \ndegrees above normal and these will be the warmest temperatures \nsince the March heat wave. Some isolated locales mainly across \nwestern Pima County will have high levels of HeatRisk on Monday, and \na few record highs will be in jeopardy as well. Some west to \nnorthwest breezes today, especially in the Upper Gila River Valley \nwith gusts 20 to 30 mph there, then lighter winds Sunday. \nThereafter, on Monday, an east push aided by surface high pressure \nextending from the southern Plains into New Mexico will result in an \nuptick in easterly breezes, especially Monday morning with the \nstrongest winds in Graham/Cochise Counties where a few spots may see \ngusts around 30 to 35 mph. Winds will taper off Monday afternoon. \n\nAs the mid/upper level ridging nudges east, that will allow a \nsoutheast to southerly flow aloft early to mid next week with an \nincrease in mid level moisture. This kind of setup may result in some \nvirga or dry thunderstorms, especially around Tuesday/Wednesday, \nwith the main area being east of Tucson. By Thursday and Friday as a \nshortwave trough passes to our north, the flow will become \nsouthwesterly again pushing out any mid level moisture. Temperatures \nwill nudge down slightly for mid to late next week but still remain \nwell above normal at around 3 to 7 degrees above normal.",
